WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
We followed two chains of references from the declaration
named riemannHypothesis in Alpöge’s Lean source.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:15.300
In the proof, the first enters a volume identity and the second
a compatibility condition. This video follows each one in.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:23.867
In the definition of HeckeAt, rd g is the value being specified.
On the right, ρ.hecke applies the Hecke operator to rd f.

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:31.267
That reference gives one edge from HeckeAt to Setup.hecke.
Watch where it sits in the two paths we traced.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:39.467
The highlighted reference ends the blue path. Both paths start
at riemannHypothesis and share their first eight links.

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:47.367
The two paths shown have 15 and 19 links. What does
each definition contribute to the argument?

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:54.167
In part 1, the masses of A and B filled a bar:
1/4 + 1/6 = 5/12.

00:00:54.167 --> 00:01:03.367
The proof has the same picture with its own data: a volume cut
into one piece per double-coset class, each of size 1 ÷ d.

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:09.867
The theorem volumes_eq_masses_of_unfold uses that decomposition:
the volume equals the sum of the reciprocal symmetry orders.

00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:15.433
That sum is classMass. In the proof, mass identifies a volume.

00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:21.633
Keep A’s three neighbours in view. Counting the B destinations
gives the matrix entry 2.

00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:30.833
Now read the function at the same neighbours. Its values are
2, −3 and −3. They give the new value at A, which is −4.

00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:38.233
HeckeAt requires the target value to agree with the normalized
neighbour sum for every certified local input.

00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:45.633
The series construction copies the setup. The lemma heckeTieT
carries the same equality across to the copy.

00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:51.533
So that is where the two sums enter the proof.
One identifies a volume.

00:01:51.533 --> 00:02:03.833
The other specifies the value of a transformed form.
The same condition holds in the copied setup.
